        "chapterId": 300,
        "headingId": 374,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:03:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 462,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64416,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 300,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Endocervicosis is most commonly identified in the bladder, but when present in the genital tract, it most commonly involves the anterior outer cervix {26825004; 11109160}. It may also occur in the peritoneum.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:03:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64417,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 300,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "<p>Typically, patients are of reproductive age and present with pelvic pain {26825004}, or they may be asymptomatic.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-08T18:09:41"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64418,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 300,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>There are insufficient epidemiological data.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:03:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64419,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 300,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:03:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64420,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 300,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The frequent history of caesarean section suggests that prior surgery may cause displacement of endocervical epithelium into the outer cervical wall.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:03:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64421,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 300,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Endocervicosis of the uterine cervix is typically a nodular mass or cysts involving the anterior cervical wall.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:03:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64422,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 300,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Endocervicosis is composed of variably sized and shaped glands, often dilated, involving the outer cervix or beyond (urinary bladder wall) and discontinuous with the superficial endocervical mucinous glands. The glands are lined by benign endocervical-type epithelium, with either a columnar or a flattened appearance. Mitoses are rare to absent. A stromal reaction, in the form of oedema and mild inflammation, may be seen with mucin extravasation {11109160}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:03:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64423,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 300,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:03:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
